  "summary": "The `terminal_stage` approach from prior rounds is the right call, but I'd refine the implementation strategy after reading the actual code. In `_run_pipeline_impl` (phases.py:1473), the pipeline is already structured as three sequential blocks gated by stage checks: (1) planning loop at line 1485, (2) spec writing + review at line 1578, and (3) impl/test/review at line 1615. A `terminal_stage` field in board config (loaded via `_get_per_board_config` in config.py:351) would require exactly one check point per block boundary — after the planning block completes (around line 1576), check if `terminal_stage == 'reviewing-plan'` and if so route to `final-human-approval`; after the spec block completes (around line 1613), check if `terminal_stage == 'spec-writing'` and route similarly. This is ~10 lines of code in `_run_pipeline_impl` plus a config field. The key insight the prior rounds missed is that the `ideating` phase is handled completely separately (in the ideation debate system, not in `_run_pipeline_impl`), so `terminal_stage` only needs to gate within the main pipeline — an ideation-only board would set `terminal_stage` to `ideating` and the pipeline simply never runs for those items, which is already the current behavior for items sitting in the ideating phase. The per-board config mechanism already exists (`_get_per_board_config` at config.py:351) but currently only supports `code_path`; adding `terminal_stage` there keeps it board-scoped without polluting the global config. One addition worth considering: a `skip_phases` list (e.g., `['ideating']`) for boards that want to skip the front of the pipeline rather than truncate the back — this handles the 'skip ideation, go straight to planning' use case that `terminal_stage` alone doesn't address, since ideation happens before the item enters the pipeline."
